{{NZelectorateShortDesc|current=yes|type=general|region=Auckland}}
'''Upper Harbour''' is a parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] in [[Auckland]] that returns one member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. It was first formed for the {{NZ election link|2014}}. The seat was won by [[New Zealand National Party|National]]'s [[Paula Bennett]] in both the 2014 and {{NZ election link|2017}}. The seat was then held by [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]]'s [[Vanushi Walters]] in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]]. The seat is currently held by [[New Zealand National Party|National's]] [[Cameron Brewer]] since the 2023 general election.

==Geography==
Upper Harbour covers the northwestern reaches of [[Waitematā Harbour]]. It stretches from [[Massey, New Zealand|Massey]], in [[West Auckland, New Zealand|West Auckland]], through [[West Harbour, Auckland|West Harbour]] and [[Hobsonville]], and across to [[Greenhithe, New Zealand|Greenhithe]] and on to [[Glenfield, New Zealand|Glenfield]] and [[Unsworth Heights]] on the [[North Shore City|North Shore]].<ref>{{cite news|last1=McQuillan|first1=Laura|last2=Marwick|first2=Felix|date=21 November 2013|title=Sweeping changes to electorates|work=[[Newstalk ZB]]|url=http://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/auckland/news/308043721-new-seats-bad-news-for-bennett|url-status=dead|access-date=21 November 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140113002217/http://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/auckland/news/308043721-new-seats-bad-news-for-bennett|archive-date=2014-01-13}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-10-17|title=Election 2020: Human rights lawyer Vanushi Walters is new Upper Harbour MP|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300131315/election-2020-human-rights-lawyer-vanushi-walters-is-new-upper-harbour-mp|access-date=2020-10-24|website=Stuff}}</ref>

==History==
Upper Harbour was proposed in the 2013/14 electorate boundary review and confirmed by the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] on 17 April 2014.<ref name="Electoral Commission">{{cite web |title=New electorate boundaries finalised|url= http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/new-electorate-boundaries-finalised-0 |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=19 April 2014 |year=2014}}</ref> The [[2013 New Zealand census|2013 census]] showed that the [[Auckland region]] had seen faster population growth than other areas, so needed an extra electorate to keep all electorates within five percent of their quota. To achieve this, the Electoral Commission abolished {{NZ electorate link|Waitakere}} and established two new electorates, Upper Harbour and {{NZ electorate link|Kelston}}.<ref name="Electoral Commission" />

When the draft changes to electorate boundaries were first announced, the incumbent of the Waitakere electorate, Paula Bennett, was quick to announce that she would stand in Upper Harbour instead. This was to prevent [[Colin Craig]] of the [[Conservative Party of New Zealand|Conservative Party]] making a claim for the electorate, as at the time, there was speculation whether the National Party would make a deal with the Conservatives for a safe seat in line with the agreement with [[ACT New Zealand]] in the {{NZ electorate link|Epsom}} electorate.<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/dominion-post/news/politics/9429898/Bennett-won-t-make-way-for-Craig |title= Bennett won't make way for Craig |first= Vernon |last= Small |newspaper= [[The Dominion Post (Wellington)|The Dominion Post]] |publisher= [[Fairfax New Zealand]] |date= 22 November 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20140609064000/http://www.stuff.co.nz/dominion-post/news/politics/9429898/Bennett-won-t-make-way-for-Craig |archive-date= 9 June 2014 |url-status= live |access-date= 22 November 2013 |df= dmy-all }}</ref> Bennett won the 2014 election with a majority of nearly 10,000 votes over [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]]'s Hermann Retzlaff.<ref name="Upper Harbour 2014 election results" />

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Upper Harbour.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
A small portion of the electorate around Tihema Stream was moved to the new electorate of [[Kaipara ki Mahurangi]] in the 2020 redistribution.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> At the {{NZ election link|2020}}, Bennett retired and Labour's [[Vanushi Walters]] won the seat against National’s [[Jake Bezzant]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-10-17|title=Election 2020: Human rights lawyer Vanushi Walters is new Upper Harbour MP|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300131315/election-2020-human-rights-lawyer-vanushi-walters-is-new-upper-harbour-mp|access-date=2020-10-24|website=Stuff}}</ref> This was one of fifteen electorates that Labour took from National in an election that dramatically changed the composition of New Zealand’s parliament.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-10-18|title=The red tide: Labour wins 15 seats held by National|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/428600/the-red-tide-labour-wins-15-seats-held-by-national|access-date=2020-10-24|website=[[RNZ]] }}</ref>

The 2025 Boundary Review saw Upper Harbour shifted northwards to accommodate population changes in northern [[Auckland]], gaining the suburbs of [[Whenuapai]] and [[Westgate, New Zealand|Westgate]] from [[Kaipara ki Mahurangi]] and [[Schnapper Rock]] from {{NZ electorate link|East Coast Bays}}, and losing [[Massey, New Zealand|Massey]] and [[Wairau Valley, Auckland|Wairau Valley]] to {{NZ electorate link|Henderson}} and {{NZ electorate link|Northcote}} respectively.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=3 September 2025 |pages=18–19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

